finish('p-versus-bpp-total-decision-problems',
 id='TCS-0003',title='Can randomness always be removed from polynomial-time decision algorithms?',
 area='Pseudorandomness and derandomization',criterion='models',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Does P=BPP? Precisely, for every language L for which a randomized Turing machine R runs in polynomial time on every input and every random tape, and satisfies $\Pr[R(x)=1_L(x)]\ge2/3$ for every binary string x, does there exist a deterministic polynomial-time Turing machine deciding L correctly on every x? The polynomial time bound of the deterministic machine may depend on R.''',
 definitions=r'''Input length is n=|x|. A polynomial-time bound means C(n+1)^k steps for fixed constants C,k, independent of x and of the random bits. R uses independent unbiased random bits, at most one fresh bit per computation step, and outputs a single bit. Its error may be on either yes or no inputs, but is bounded separately on each input. P consists of deterministic polynomial-time decision languages and BPP of languages meeting the randomized guarantee above. These are total decision problems, with no excluded promise inputs. No advice, oracle, quantum operations, or input distribution is allowed. The requested deterministic simulation may use a different algorithm; it need not reproduce R's random choices, preserve its precise exponent, or be generated by an effective compiler from R.''',
 answer_criterion='Prove that every BPP language has a deterministic polynomial-time decider, or exhibit a BPP language and prove that none exists. A subexponential simulation, a simulation using advice, or correctness only on most inputs does not settle the question. Proving the stronger derandomization claim for all promise problems would suffice, but is not required here.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Randomized decision algorithms may use randomness to avoid exceptional choices or to test a property through a small sample. Their guarantee is pointwise: even the worst input must receive the correct answer with high probability. Repeating an algorithm reduces its error but leaves it randomized. The question asks whether every such use of randomness can ultimately be replaced by a deterministic polynomial computation.'),
  paragraph('One simple approach is to run the randomized algorithm on every possible random tape and take the majority answer. That is correct, but takes exponential time when the tape has polynomial length. A suitable pseudorandom generator would reduce the search to a polynomial-size set of tapes, constructed efficiently. The hard part is ensuring that this small set fools the computation on every input.', 'iw'),
  paragraph('A nonuniform version of this idea already works: after error amplification, an averaging argument supplies one polynomial-length advice string that works for every input of a given length. This proves BPP is contained in P/poly. The string can depend on the input length without an efficient procedure to find it, which is exactly why that inclusion does not answer the uniform question.', 'book'),
  paragraph('Strong circuit hardness provides a conditional route to full derandomization. Impagliazzo and Wigderson show that an exponentially circuit-hard function computable in exponential time suffices. The condition is substantially more specific than merely P≠NP. Current work also studies the amount of slowdown and the structure of the randomized algorithm, producing finer conditional results without an unconditional equality proof.', 'recent'),
  paragraph('This card concerns computing one correct decision bit. Sampling a distribution, producing one of many valid witnesses, or deciding only inputs with a promise leads to related but different formulations. Keeping these tasks separate prevents a derandomization theorem for one setting from being silently substituted for another.')],
 why='This determines whether random bits add power to efficient total decision computation. It is a central organizing question for pseudorandom generators, explicit constructions, and the connection between circuit lower bounds and algorithm design.',
 importance=dict(score=97,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='The main general derandomization question for polynomial-time decision algorithms, with broad consequences for the role of randomness and for hardness-versus-randomness techniques.'),
 progress=[
  step('1978','Adleman establishes BPP⊆P/poly. Suitable fixed random advice exists for each input length, but uniform polynomial-time computation of that advice is not supplied.','book'),
  step('1997','Impagliazzo and Wigderson prove P=BPP if there is a Boolean function computable in time 2^(O(n)) that requires circuits of size 2^(c n), for some c>0 and all sufficiently large n. The circuit-hardness assumption remains essential to this theorem.','iw'),
  step('2026-05','Doron, Moshkovitz, Oh, and Zuckerman improve conditional pseudorandomness parameters for algorithms satisfying an insensitivity property of their correct and incorrect random tapes. This concerns finer generators for a specified algorithm class, not unconditional removal of randomness from all BPP computations.','recent'),
  step('2026-07-19','Version 9 of a preprint by Tianrong Lin claims the opposite conclusion P≠BPP, along with stronger separations. This review has not verified that construction or located independent primary validation. The claimed separation is recorded without treating it as established.','claim')],
 references=[
  ref('book','Mathematics and Computation','Avi Wigderson',2018,'https://www.math.ias.edu/files/mathandcomp.pdf','March 2018 draft, Theorem 7.4, Open Problem 7.5, and Sections 7.1–7.3'),
  ref('iw','P=BPP unless E has sub-exponential circuits: Derandomizing the XOR Lemma','Russell Impagliazzo; Avi Wigderson',1997,'https://www.math.ias.edu/~avi/PUBLICATIONS/MYPAPERS/IW97/proc.pdf','STOC 1997; published title P=BPP if E Requires Exponential Circuits, DOI 10.1145/258533.258590'),
  ref('recent','Pseudorandomness Beating the Hybrid Argument for Insensitive Algorithms','Dean Doron; Dana Moshkovitz; Justin Oh; David Zuckerman',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/082/','Revision 1, 17 May 2026; abstract and introduction'),
  ref('claim','Probabilistic Computers (and Hence Quantum Computers) Are Rigorously More Powerful Than Classical Deterministic Computers, and Derandomization','Tianrong Lin',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09549v9','Unverified direct claim; version 9, 19 July 2026; original submission August 2023')],
 status='uncertain',status_note='P=BPP remains the target of mainstream derandomization work cited in May 2026. A July 2026 revision of a preprint claims separation; this review has not established its correctness or independent validation. The card is not marked resolved. Status search performed through 10 September 2026.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
